## clasp (v3.3.0, verified from google/clasp 2026-08-06)

- `npm install -g @google/clasp`; Apps Script API ON at
  <https://script.google.com/home/usersettings>; `clasp login`
- `push` / `pull` kept their v2 names; `clone`→`clone-script`, `status`→`show-file-status`,
  `deploy`→`create-deployment`
- **`clasp pull` writes `.js` by default** — the first entry in `scriptExtensions`. Fixed by
  setting `[".gs", ".js"]`, otherwise you end up with both `Code.js` and `Code.gs`.
- **`clasp push` deletes remote files with no local counterpart.** The project should hold
  exactly the four pushed files.
- Never invent `appsscript.json` — a wrong manifest overwrites the live one and can change
  the web app's `executeAs` / `access` or break the deployment.

## Windows setup notes (learned the hard way)

- Node wasn't installed. `winget install OpenJS.NodeJS.LTS` (v24.19.0) worked, but VS Code
  needed a **full restart** — not just a new terminal — to see it on PATH.
- PowerShell blocks `.ps1` by default, so `node.exe` ran while `npm` didn't
  ("running scripts is disabled"). Fixed with
  `Set-ExecutionPolicy -Scope CurrentUser RemoteSigned`. Would have hit `clasp` too.
- The API toggle on the settings page sits at the far right of the row and is easy to miss.
- The GitHub repo already had a manual upload of the same three files, so the first
  `git pull --rebase` hit add/add conflicts. Resolved with
  `git checkout --theirs <files>` (during a rebase `--theirs` = the commit being replayed,
  i.e. the local ones) and `git -c core.editor=true rebase --continue` to skip vim.

